workedExample:
1. The runs are already sorted, so the only question for each output record is "which run head is smallest right now?".
2. A min-heap of the 1,024 heads answers that with a pop and a push, each moving an item at most about 10 levels, because a heap of 1,024 items has height log₂ 1024 = 10.
3. Compare the alternatives per record:
   - heap merge: about 10 comparison levels;
   - full re-sort: about 30, because it ignores the existing runs;
   - scanning all heads: about 1,024;
   - merging runs one after another: early records are re-copied up to about 1,000 times.
4. So the heap merge wins, at about ten comparison steps per record (up to a small constant factor, since a pop compares two children per level).

In practice, buffered reads from each run matter as much as comparisons; whether I/O or comparison work dominates depends on the storage system.
